WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990
Build the capstone in milestones.

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:05.648
First run the two local services and explain their contracts.

00:00:05.798 --> 00:00:11.216
Then add order lines and query-performance tests, run PostgreSQL migrations, add authentication

00:00:11.216 --> 00:00:16.635
and ownership checks, implement the outbox relay and consumer, and extend the

00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:18.893
workflow with payment and compensation.

00:00:19.043 --> 00:00:23.363
Finish with observability, load measurement, and a failure demonstration.

00:00:23.513 --> 00:00:30.294
In interviews, begin with functional requirements, scale assumptions, consistency needs, and failure

00:00:30.294 --> 00:00:30.860
tolerance.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.870
Draw the data ownership and transaction boundaries.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.175
Trace one request, one concurrent conflict, one timeout, and one restart.

00:00:39.325 --> 00:00:42.462
Explain why you chose a design and what evidence would cause you

00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:43.247
to change it.

00:00:43.397 --> 00:00:46.465
Do not present every extension as already implemented.

00:00:46.615 --> 00:00:51.220
A strong portfolio demonstration shows two clients buying the last item, a

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.825
duplicate checkout returning the same order, an inventory timeout leaving a pending

00:00:55.825 --> 00:00:59.662
order, and a recovered retry confirming it without reserving twice.

00:00:59.812 --> 00:01:06.113
Include test output, commands, schema migrations, and a concise architecture decision record.

00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:12.508
Run a 45-minute mock interview: five minutes requirements, ten architecture and schema,

00:01:12.508 --> 00:01:18.753
ten concurrency and transactions, ten distributed failures, and ten security, operations, and

00:01:18.753 --> 00:01:19.274
tradeoffs.

00:01:19.424 --> 00:01:25.095
Score correctness, clarity, data ownership, failure handling, and evidence from one to

00:01:25.095 --> 00:01:25.568
five.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:29.756
Any answer claiming that a timeout proves failure or that an outbox

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.438
prevents all duplicates needs revision.

00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:34.902
Use a runnable experiment to resolve your weakest assumption.

00:01:35.052 --> 00:01:35.723
Question.

00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:39.109
How do you choose between a monolith and microservices?

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:39.836
Answer.

00:01:39.986 --> 00:01:46.325
Compare team ownership, deployment independence, scaling needs, domain stability, and operational cost;

00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:50.551
choose the simplest architecture that satisfies the constraints.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:51.372
Question.

00:01:51.522 --> 00:01:53.859
What makes an advanced answer convincing?

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:54.585
Answer.

00:01:54.735 --> 00:02:00.803
Precise invariants, explicit transaction and failure boundaries, measured tradeoffs, and a recovery

00:02:00.803 --> 00:02:02.826
story supported by tests.
